          "text": "Good — this is a clear, well-scoped problem. Here's the feasibility analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Feasibility Analysis: Document Processing Service\n\n### 1. System Boundaries\n\n```\nMobile App (future) ──► REST/gRPC API ──► Document Processing Service\n                                              │\n                                    ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n                                    │         │         │\n                                  OCR       Validation  Storage\n                                  Engine    Engine      Layer\n                                            │\n                                    ┌───────┴───────┐\n                                    │               │\n                              AR Rules Module   BR Rules Module\n                              (Argentina)       (Brazil, etc.)\n```\n\nIn scope: Image ingestion → OCR → regional validation → persistence + image storage.\nOut of scope: Mobile/web app (just the API contract).\n\n### 2. Feasibility — Core Concerns\n\n| Concern | Assessment | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OCR quality** | Open-source OCR (Tesseract, EasyOCR, PaddleOCR, Surya) is mature for printed text. Handwritten tickets are harder but improving. | Medium |\n| **Spanish/Argentine receipts** | Tesseract has Spanish trained data. EasyOCR + PaddleOCR support Spanish well. Fine-tuning may be needed for receipt-specific layouts. | Low-Medium |\n| **Pluggable regional rules** | Classic Strategy/Plugin pattern. Well-understood design, no technical blockers. | Low |\n| **Async processing** | OCR is CPU-bound and slow (1-10s per image). Must be async — queue-based or background workers. | **Must design for this** |\n| **Image storage + DB consistency** | Object store (MinIO/S3) + relational DB (PostgreSQL). Standard pattern, no novelty. | Low |\n\n### 3. Tech Stack Options (Open Source First)\n\n**OCR Engine:**\n\n| Engine | Language | Spanish Support | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tesseract** | C++ (bindings everywhere) | Good (spa.traineddata) | Most mature, battle-tested |\n| **EasyOCR** | Python | Good (80+ langs) | Simpler API, decent accuracy |\n| **PaddleOCR** | Python | Very good | Higher accuracy, more complex setup |\n| **Surya** | Python | Good | Newer, strong on receipts |\n\n**Service Language — 3 viable paths:**\n\n| Stack | OCR Story | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Python (FastAPI)** | First-class | Richest OCR ecosystem, fast dev | GIL, slower runtime, deployment size |\n| **Go** | Call external OCR via subprocess/HTTP | Fast, simple deployments, great concurrency | Must shell out or wrap OCR as sidecar |\n| **Rust (Axum/Actix)** | Bindings to Tesseract C API | Performance, safety, small footprint | Steeper dev curve, fewer OCR libs |\n\n**Recommendation:** Python (FastAPI) for the service, with PaddleOCR or EasyOCR. It's the pragmatic choice for an OCR-heavy service — no need to prematurely optimize for performance when the bottleneck is OCR itself.\n\n### 4. Architecture — Async Processing\n\n```\nPOST /documents  ──►  Store image (MinIO) + Create DB record (status=pending)\n                     ──►  Enqueue OCR job (Redis/DB-backed queue)\n                          ──►  Worker picks job → OCR → Validate (regional rules) →\n                               Update DB (status=done, extracted_data) + persist metadata\nGET  /documents/{id} ──► Return status + data\n```\n\nKey decisions to lock in:\n- **Queue:** Redis (simple) or PostgreSQL `SKIP LOCKED` (no extra infra)\n- **Storage:** MinIO (S3-compatible, self-hostable)\n- **DB:** PostgreSQL (JSONB for extracted data, stable, open source)\n\n### 5. Pluggable Regional Validation Design\n\n```python\n# Each region implements:\nclass RegionValidator(ABC):\n    region_code: str          # \"AR\", \"BR\"...\n    def validate_invoice(self, data: dict) -> ValidationResult\n    def validate_ticket(self, data: dict) -> ValidationResult\n    def validate_payment(self, data: dict) -> ValidationResult\n```\n\nValidation rules for Argentina (starting example):\n- CUIT/CUIL format checks\n- AFIP invoice number patterns (CAE/CAEA)\n- IVA breakdown validation\n- Date format conventions (DD/MM/YYYY)\n\nModules are discovered at startup, hot-pluggable, no code changes needed to add a new region.\n\n### 6. BDD/TDD Fit\n\n- **BDD:** Gherkin feature files for API behavior (\"Given an uploaded invoice image, When OCR processes it, Then extracted fields match expected values\")\n- **TDD:** Unit tests for each validation rule, unit tests for the OCR pipeline stages\n- **Framework:** `behave` or `pytest-bdd` (Python), both open source\n- OCR testing challenge: need real/fixture images. Feasible with curated test images.\n\n### 7. Multi-Agent SDD Pipeline (opencode native)\n\n```\nPhase 1: Constitution → Agent: Architecture reviewer\nPhase 2: Spec → Agent: Spec writer (generates detailed spec doc)\nPhase 3: Plan → Agent: Task planner (breaks spec into tasks)\nPhase 4: Tasks → Agents (parallel): Implementation agents per task\nPhase 5: Validation → Agent: Test runner + reporter\n```\n\nEach phase WAITS for HITL confirmation before proceeding. AI agents only do `git status/log/diff` — you handle commits.\n\n### 8. Risks to Address in Spec\n\n1. **OCR accuracy on low-quality photos** — need image pre-processing (deskew, contrast) + confidence thresholds\n2. **Layout variance** — different merchants format receipts differently; may need template matching or LLM-based extraction\n3. **Rate limiting** — OCR is expensive; need to plan for concurrent limits\n4. **Data privacy** — receipts may contain PII; need to define retention and encryption policies\n\n---\n\n## Next Step: Constitution\n\nIf this analysis passes your bar, the next SDD phase is the **Constitution** — a document that sets the governing principles: architecture decisions, constraints, non-negotiables, and stack choices.\n\nDoes this feasibility analysis raise any concerns, or should I move to drafting the Constitution?",
